# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# EXP-1: the explored overlay draws inside the squares the party has
# walked, and nowhere else on the screen.
#
# **A pair leg, and it can be one.** This seam claims no key — it is a
# setting, not a command (`seam_explored.cpp`) — so every keystroke in
# the script below reaches the program in both halves and the two runs
# differ by the flag alone. That is the one thing the journal's legs
# could not have (#234).
#
# The party is on the wilderness travel view from frame 9,552, which the
# first `allow` covers in full: **on, the overworld shown, and nothing
# walked but the square under the party's feet, the two runs are byte for
# byte the same screen.** That is the stronger of this seam's two
# fidelity claims, as a picture, and it holds only because the square the
# party is standing on is never marked.
#
# From the first step the two rects below are the only pixels that may
# differ, and they are the two squares the party has left behind. They
# have fixed screen positions for this walk because the window scrolls
# with the party: at every frame after the first step the party is the
# middle cell of the five and the squares it came from are the two below
# it. Measured on a real run (M5-E5d, #256).
about the trail is drawn inside the squares the party walked, and nowhere else
kind pair
program START.EXE
both code-wheel
# The code-wheel bypass is gated on the wheel itself (#115), and both
# sides of this pair drive past the challenge, so both need it. Pinned
# by digest because a document never enters this tree.
document 0db301aeac4d2ec1e63b409ca6d3c9d39c63381d6712ff2ce7edc0528c6586fd
seam explored
until 260000000
every 25
# A screen may appear in the two runs a dump apart, because a repaint
# lands where it lands. One dump of slack, the same as every other leg.
slack 1

# Slot J: the one shipped save whose party is already standing on a
# wilderness area (`docs/explored-overlay.md` §4). Eight steps north,
# 150 frames apart, which is the cadence every driven walk in this tree
# uses.
key A@7601
key Return@7651
key L@8951
key J@9201
key Up@10600
key Up@10750
key Up@10900
key Up@11050
key Up@11200
key Up@11350
key Up@11500
key Up@11650

# The square the party left on its last step, and the one before that:
# window column 2, rows 3 and 4, at 24 pixels a cell from (8, 8).
rect behind 56,80,79,103
rect behind2 56,104,79,127

# The whole run up to the first step, the arrival on the wilderness map
# included: not one pixel.
allow 0-10650 none
# One square walked and left behind.
allow 10675-10800 behind
# And from the second step on, two — which is all the window can show of
# a trail that is walking off the top of it.
allow 10825-end behind behind2
